Subject: winhttp: Report certificate rejection reasons without weakening validation.

Based on Wine 36b6a2cf679fb395f668a917b76537190e212d9c.

The runtime 0.2 scratch tests reject untrusted, expired and hostname-mismatched
certificates, but report ERROR_WINHTTP_SECURE_CHANNEL_ERROR without a certificate
failure callback. Preserve the validator's error and allocation failures instead
of overwriting them with the generic channel error. Translate only the seven
certificate errors to a DWORD WINHTTP_CALLBACK_STATUS_SECURE_FAILURE reason and
return ERROR_WINHTTP_SECURE_FAILURE. Credential acquisition and the existing
async REQUEST_ERROR notification path are unchanged. Explicit transport and
allocation errors are preserved; other SSPI failures remain channel errors.

Release a rejected certificate chain even though the connection never became
secure, and free any remaining SSPI output token on handshake exit, including
send failure. Detach and release the failed connection and free its address
before invoking the reentrant callback through send_callback, which preserves
the notification mask and request context.

This is a diagnostic compatibility fix: certificate rejection is maintained.
Security ignore options and SSL validation policy are unchanged. The combined
certificate-error ignore ladder is a separate backlog issue.

Regression coverage is in tests/network.c and tests/test_network_runtime.py.
After compiling both WinHTTP DLL architectures and integrating them into a
disposable runtime overlay, run from the MacTMNF repository root:

    MACTMNF_TEST_RUNTIME=/absolute/path/to/patched-runtime python3 tests/test_network_runtime.py

Synchronous and asynchronous controls and rejection cases pass with isolated
rebuilt DLLs for i686 and x86_64, loaded app-locally inside disposable prefixes.
Async checks cover callback count, context, payload size, ordering and terminal
REQUEST_ERROR. This does not qualify a complete runtime. Callback close/retry,
allocation failures and the remaining certificate-error categories still need
runtime coverage and native Windows comparison.

